import{_ as a,o as t,c as n,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.RycU2wFZ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Agent Payment Flow","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"learn/02-agent-payments/agent-payment-flow.md","filePath":"learn/02-agent-payments/agent-payment-flow.md","lastUpdated":1792595354000}'),s={name:"learn/02-agent-payments/agent-payment-flow.md"};function i(o,e,l,p,c,h){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(`<h1 id="the-agent-payment-flow" tabindex="-1">The Agent Payment Flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-agent-payment-flow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Agent Payment Flow&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="a-practical-scenario" tabindex="-1">A practical scenario <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-practical-scenario" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A practical scenario&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Suppose a user asks an agent to research market data and prepare a report. A data API provides the required information for 0.01 USDC per query. If the agent is connected to a payment system, it may purchase the data automatically when the request meets its authorization rules.</p><p>A complete payment flow involves more than sending a transaction. It can include requesting a resource, receiving payment terms, evaluating policy, reserving budget, creating authorization, verifying payment information, delivering the resource, settling, updating state, and recovering from errors. Exact ordering varies by protocol and implementation. The outline below uses an illustrative x402 buyer, gateway, and facilitator architecture.</p><h2 id="overview" tabindex="-1">Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Overview&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Agent → Buyer SDK → Paid API request</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ← HTTP 402 payment requirement</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → Parse requirements and evaluate policy</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → Atomically reserve budget</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → Create payment authorization</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → Retry request with payment information</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Gateway verifies payment → calls upstream service → delivers resource</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Settlement is submitted or tracked → state is updated and reconciled</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Payment authorization, resource delivery, and settlement are related, but they are not interchangeable events.</p><h2 id="_1-request-the-resource" tabindex="-1">1. Request the resource <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-request-the-resource" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Request the resource&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The agent first makes an ordinary HTTP request, for example:</p><div class="language-http vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">http</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">GET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> /v1/market-data</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">Host</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> api.example.com</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The client may not know whether the resource is paid. The server may return the resource directly or respond with payment requirements.</p><h2 id="_2-receive-and-inspect-the-payment-requirement" tabindex="-1">2. Receive and inspect the payment requirement <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-receive-and-inspect-the-payment-requirement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Receive and inspect the payment requirement&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An API that requires payment may return HTTP 402 with terms such as the service, price, network, asset, and recipient. These fields are illustrative, not a wire-format specification.</p><p>The client must check the requirements against its own policy. It should reject a request if, for example, the network is not allowed or the recipient is not an approved provider. It must not blindly accept arbitrary terms supplied by a server.</p><h2 id="_3-evaluate-policy" tabindex="-1">3. Evaluate policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-evaluate-policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Evaluate policy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An illustrative policy might set a 0.10 USDC per-payment limit, a 2.00 USDC daily budget, and an allowlist containing <code>market-data</code>. A 0.01 USDC request can proceed only if the provider, amount, remaining budget, and request rate all pass their checks. A low price does not make an unapproved merchant acceptable.</p><p>Passing policy means the request may continue; it does not mean funds have settled.</p><h2 id="_4-reserve-budget-atomically" tabindex="-1">4. Reserve budget atomically <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-reserve-budget-atomically" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Reserve budget atomically&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Suppose 0.05 USDC remains and two requests each need 0.04 USDC. If both merely read the balance, both could proceed. An atomic reservation gives the first request 0.04 USDC and leaves 0.01 USDC available, so the second is rejected.</p><p>Reservation temporarily holds capacity for a request. The system later converts it to spend or releases it based on the outcome. The implementation must use a concurrency-safe transaction or equivalent mechanism.</p><h2 id="_5-create-payment-authorization" tabindex="-1">5. Create payment authorization <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-create-payment-authorization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Create payment authorization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>After budget approval, the client creates authorization appropriate to the selected payment scheme. On some EVM paths this may use an EIP-712 structured signature and an EIP-3009 authorization.</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Payment requirement</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ↓ validate network, asset, recipient, and amount</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Build authorization data</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Wallet or signer signs</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Payment credential</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A signature proves that a key authorized particular data. It does not replace budget controls, merchant restrictions, or business rules. Before signing, the client should verify that the signed fields match the original payment requirement.</p><h2 id="_6-submit-and-verify-payment-information" tabindex="-1">6. Submit and verify payment information <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-submit-and-verify-payment-information" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Submit and verify payment information&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The client sends payment information as required by the protocol. The service or payment component should validate the signature, authorization conditions, asset, network, amount, recipient, expiry, and whether the authorization has already been used. The exact checks depend on the payment scheme. A client assertion that it has paid is not proof of verified payment.</p><h2 id="_7-deliver-the-resource" tabindex="-1">7. Deliver the resource <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-deliver-the-resource" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Deliver the resource&quot;">​</a></h2><p>After payment verification, the provider returns the requested resource. The system should record fulfillment separately from payment and settlement so an upstream service failure does not cause the client to pay again blindly.</p><h2 id="_8-submit-or-track-settlement" tabindex="-1">8. Submit or track settlement <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-submit-or-track-settlement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. Submit or track settlement&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Some flows submit settlement before delivery; others may verify authorization first and track settlement asynchronously. A network timeout cannot establish whether settlement failed. Preserve a pending or unknown result, query the same operation, and reconcile before retrying.</p><h2 id="_9-update-state-and-recover" tabindex="-1">9. Update state and recover <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-update-state-and-recover" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Update state and recover&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Record policy outcome, reservation, authorization, settlement, and fulfillment as distinct facts. If settlement is confirmed but fulfillment failed, recover delivery. If the payment outcome is unknown, reconcile it before creating another payment. Idempotency keys help ensure that repeated requests for the same business intent do not create duplicate effects.</p><h2 id="key-takeaway" tabindex="-1">Key takeaway <a class="header-anchor" href="#key-takeaway" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Key takeaway&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Treat the flow as coordinated stages with explicit state transitions. Policy approval, cryptographic authorization, payment verification, settlement, and service delivery each answer a different question.</p>`,34)])])}const m=a(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
